<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu al-Misk Kafur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/905">905</a>–<a href="/years/968">968</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Kafur al-Ikhshidi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu al-Misk Kafur was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> of African origin, once a slave, who became ruler of Ikhshidid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-tughj-al-ikhshid">al-Ikhshid</a> died in 946 he governed as regent for his two sons and from 966 in his own name, holding Egypt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the Hijaz. The poet al-Mutanabbi first praised and then satirised him. Soon after his death in 968 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> conquered Egypt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-al-misk-kafur.jpg" alt="Abu al-Misk Kafur" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinar_of_Abu%27l-Musk_Kafur_al-Ikhshidi,_AH_355.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
